🔹 Course Overview
This course focuses on computer networks, network attacks, network security controls, and monitoring & incident response.
It builds a strong foundation for SOC Analyst and Cybersecurity Analyst roles.

✅ Module 1: Network Architecture & Communication
🔹 What is a Computer Network?
  A computer network is a collection of interconnected devices (computers, servers, routers) that communicate to share data and resources.

🔹 Why Networks Matter in Security
   Most cyber attacks happen over networks
   Understanding networks helps identify attack paths
   Helps in placing correct security controls

🔹 OSI Model (7 Layers)
Layer	Name	        Function	                          Security Relevance
7	Application      	User interaction (HTTP, FTP)	      Phishing, malware
6	Presentation	    Encryption & formatting	            SSL/TLS
5	Session	          Session control                   	Session hijacking
4	Transport	        TCP/UDP	                            Port scanning
3	Network	          IP addressing                     	IP spoofing
2	Data Link        	MAC addressing	                    ARP poisoning
1	Physical	        Cables & signals	                  Physical attacks

🔹 Security Benefit
OSI model helps analysts quickly identify where an attack is occurring.

🔹 Common Network Protocols
HTTP/HTTPS – Web communication
DNS – Domain name resolution
FTP/SFTP – File transfer
SMTP – Email transfer
TCP/UDP – Transport protocols

🔹 Example
HTTP → vulnerable
HTTPS → encrypted & secure

✅ Module 2: Network Attacks
🔹 What is a Network Attack?
   An attempt to gain unauthorized access, disrupt services, or steal data through network communication.

🔹 Common Network Attacks
1️⃣ Man-in-the-Middle (MITM)
Attacker intercepts communication
Common in public Wi-Fi
🔹 Impact
Credential theft
Data manipulation
🔹 Mitigation
HTTPS
VPN

2️⃣ DNS Spoofing
Fake DNS responses redirect users
🔹 Impact
Phishing
Malware delivery

3️⃣ ARP Poisoning
Attacker associates their MAC with victim IP
🔹 Layer Affected
OSI Layer 2 (Data Link)

4️⃣ DoS / DDoS
Flooding network with traffic
🔹 Impact
Service outage
Financial loss
🔹 Example Risk Scenario
Asset: Web server
Threat: DDoS
Vulnerability: No traffic filtering
Impact: Website downtime

✅ Module 3: Network Security Controls
🔹 What are Network Security Controls?
   Controls designed to protect networks from unauthorized access and attacks.

🔹 Types of Network Controls
🔸 Firewalls
    Filter incoming & outgoing traffic
    Based on rules
🔸 IDS / IPS
   IDS → Detects suspicious traffic
   IPS → Detects + blocks attacks
🔸 VPN (Virtual Private Network)
   Encrypts communication
   Protects remote access
🔸 Network Segmentation
   Divides network into zones
   Limits attack spread
🔹 Example
Risk: Unauthorized access
Control: Firewall + IDS
Result: Reduced attack surface

✅ Module 4: Network Monitoring & Incident Response
🔹 What is Network Monitoring?
Continuous observation of network traffic to detect anomalies and threats.
🔹 Monitoring Tools
Wireshark – Packet analysis
IDS logs – Intrusion detection
Firewall logs – Traffic filtering
🔹 Incident Response (Network Focus)
Steps:
Detection – Alert triggered
Analysis – Identify attack type
Containment – Block attacker IP
Recovery – Restore services

🔹 Example
Alert: Suspicious traffic
Action: Block IP
Result: Attack stopped

